  {
    "episode_id": 74498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, first we have come a long way, but it was an incredibly painful lesson that we had to learn in order for us to take this threat, this vulnerability more seriously. And I think if you look back over the last 15 years, Louisiana has come in and we have recognized that there's more tools in the toolbox than just the levee or flood wall recognizing the role that the coastal system plays, whether it's barrier islands or marsh areas, Or some of our ridges engineers that help to provide that natural protection. Some of our coastal forest that we've invested in protecting and invested in their health and just really re plumbing the entire coastal area. to ensure that we have a sustainable system and just really looked at resiliency in a different light. So we've done that and made a tremendous amount of progress. And the second thing is, not just in Louisiana, but nationally, We have we have really begun this shift from an entirely reactive posture at the federal level where you come in and spend billions of dollars in the aftermath of a disaster and we're making a lot more investment on the front end being proactive in the resilience of our communities and our ecosystem and look I We still have more work to do, but a tremendous amount of progress has been made so far. And I think that we all need to be really proud of the work that's been done, but should not rest and that we have much more work to be done.",
