  {
    "episode_id": 74498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Well, look, we just talked about Hurricane Katrina and y'all are all aware of the incredible devastation that happened, whether it was fishing boats literally on top of people's houses. We all saw the pictures on top of bridges and other places. The shrimp sheds and ice houses, all the places where the fisher men and women come in to deliver their catch. Those places were destroyed just really and not to mention the people's personal homes and businesses destroyed. And so you had that, we've seen these unfair trade practices, we've seen the farmed seafood products coming in from other countries with illegal chemicals and all kinds of challenges on our seafood producers and fishers in Louisiana. And so as we're in this coronavirus pandemic, we've seen additional impacts heaped on top of them. We saw it as an opportunity to connect the dots. So you have USDA's out there providing nutrition programs and food for folks. And they're already paying for these things. They're already delivering the meals. And I mean, we sort of had this light go off. Every table needs trip, I'd say, right? And so it was this thing, hey, look, we're already buying food and they're buying proteins and others. Shrimp and Louisiana seafood is a great, it's a abundantly available. It's a fresh organic product. And so we started working with USDA back in April about incorporating shrimp into some of their nutrition programs, working with them on assistance to our craw fishermen and women. And ultimately, we were able to get a number of good wins. We had a win of a total of around $70 or $80 million in assistance to our seafood producers, and then we announced $20 million in shrimp that will be purchased by USDA in the corporate end of these nutrition programs, and then recently just announced the crawfish How crawfish producers will also be eligible for assistance from the Department of Agriculture coming up. So really just trying to help these folks. But we said again, you know, Jock Simone, it's all about connecting the dots for already providing nutrition programs. Let's get a win-win where you're helping folks that are down and providing great, nutritious, awesome food to people around the country. So it's just a win-win.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
