  {
    "episode_id": 74498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "Sure, sure. So the Water Resources Development Act, of course, is legislation that's supposed to pass every two years that authorized all the Corps of Engineers efforts and projects around the country. We passed that out of the house about three weeks ago or so and a lot of good wins in there for for Louisiana. I think just good policy. We added about a dozen things in there. And it was everything from, and this one's a little weedy, but Louisiana is paying for a lot of the hurricane protection work after Hurricane Katrina. And that debt was initially intended to be somewhere around 1.3 billion but because of some of the delays and the accruing interest that debt is expected to double or perhaps even triple over time and so what's going to happen is this is going to become an increasing drag on the state's budget and potentially on the coastal program itself. So we reach an agreement with the governor and with a number of other members of Congress that if the state committed to take their cost savings from provisions we worked on in Congress, if they committed to take the cost savings and reinvest them back into the coast, into community resiliency type projects, Then we would agree, we would all agree to pass this law that is going to provide Louisiana the opportunity to renegotiate the debt, to get some of these interests absurd or really high interest costs reduced. We also have found ways to ensure Louisiana gets credit. for other coastal work in Louisiana. At the end of the day, we think this could be valued at about a billion and a half dollars that would be in savings, that would be reinvested back into coastal resiliency, ecological resiliency. And look, let's keep in mind, every penny of that invested means savings for federal taxpayers in terms of future disaster costs and damages prevented. And so this really is a win for Louisiana, a win for our coast. And even a win for federal taxpayers. So that's in there. We're all aware of the core. And we often say behind their backs and even to their faces that the core may be slow, but at least they're expensive. And when you have the kind of critical or urgent projects we have in Louisiana, neither of those are OK. And so we passed a provision that dies both at expansion and extension of a program that will allow the state of Louisiana, the Coastal Protection Restoration Authority, our parishes, or our levy districts to actually be the lead on these Corps of Engineers projects. And so hopefully resulting in better, faster, cheaper outcomes and delivery of these projects, which is a really big deal for Louisiana. So a lot of other wins in there, but just to highlight a few, I think those are some of the ones that are going to have the most impact on our state and our ultimately on our resilience.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
