  {
    "episode_id": 74498,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Sure. So it's no secret that we have seen repeated high water marks on the Mississippi River system over the last several years. And, you know, I mean, it's just really amazing seeing these water levels. We hit a record in 2011. hit a record in 2016 and just continue operating the Bonacari for the spillway, the pressure relief out for the first time ever twice in the same year, first time ever opening it up in January, which isn't supposed to be a high-water time of the month. And the first time you maybe look at it and say, wow, that's a crazy anomaly. Second time, huh, it happened again. And when you start getting third, fourth, fifth time, it's like, wait a minute, something's going on. And so that's a trend. And so what we did is, two years ago, we put a provision in the law that directed the Corps of Engineers to look at how to manage the water from the old river control structure, which of course is north of Baton Rouge. That's where the the Old River, the Chafalaya, and Mississippi River kind of all have that confluence, and it manages that flow on the lower system. But we recognize that we actually need to look even much higher in the system, so there's a provision that looks at the upper Mississippi River system above that sort of Cape Giarda, Giardo, and Burt's New Madrid area north, and then a second provision that looks at that area south, because A lot of folks just look at their flooding problems in the water and just say, get it into the river as quick as you can. And that may solve their problems, but it presents dilemmas for us, not just in the fact that we're managing record water year after year, but also that we're seeing these dead zones in the Gulf of Mexico that are to the size of Connecticut in the area that's supposed to have the top fisheries and the highest ecological production on the continent, so those aren't compatible. So we included a provision that requires that they look at how to manage that water flow. I'm looking at other tools available that could even mean reservoirs and slowing down water in the upper system, sending it through buffer zones and riparian areas to filter out some of the nutrients that are in there, but stop all this surge of water to us and then, of course, down in our area, look at these river reconnection projects. really reiterate my river re-connection, re-establishment, re-re-re, term, but simply helping to re-establish the connection between the river and the coastal area that was there before, because that gives us more tools to manage the water and doesn't just dump everything into Lake Pontchartrain, or through the Morgansa floodway and dumping it on Morgan City and other areas that quite frankly can't handle more water. And so it just brings more tools to the tool chest that looks at it like the system that it is and it looks at it more, providing more holistic solutions. So I think it's really an important provision is going to be an important study to recommend, I think, fundamental changes in how we manage the system to benefit the coastal ecology, to benefit the seafood, to benefit the communities, to help rebuild and reestablish this delta building process. And so a very important provision that I think is going to yield some big changes.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
