    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74498.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so we secured $1.7 billion for the state of Louisiana to hand out to flood victims from our thousand-year flood we had in 2016. Only the federal government can come up with these crazy things where they say that if you got a loan you were ineligible for a grant because they considered them duplicative of one another. It was really bad policy because those people that got alone were those that were trying to recover faster and get back into their homes in their community earlier. We didn't know the grants were going to be available at that time. So when the grants became available, those that were proactive were told, you're not allowed, you're ineligible for a grant. We think that again, bad policy, we changed the law to make them eligible for those grants, just like everyone else, and the federal government, the federal bureaucracy among FEMA, the Office of Management, Budget, and HUD did an awful job implementing that law. So we've come in and added additional provisions to the appropriations bill to help do some recalibration, of course, correction there, just simply to make sure that money gets into the victims' hands of $1.7 billion. And here we are four years later, and only 700 million of it has been offered the flood victims. So just really frustrating. You have need, you have money, and we're just trying to connect the dots. Yeah,",
