    "turn_text": "Yeah, you know, Jack, we talked earlier about the governor's executive orders. We talked about Hurricane Katrina and some of the progress since then. I'll say it again, when you bring up the climate topic, it usually becomes a very divisive issue. And I think if you rather than talking about climate to sort of writ large, this big topic that macro-level issue, let's talk about the components of it. Let's talk about community resiliency. Let's talk about how to protect our coastal communities where 40% of the population lives today. And that's not partisan. We can come to agreement that we need to make sure we're protecting our communities and making these areas more resilient. When you start talking about solutions to bring down energy costs, and that may be how you talk about it in one crowd, but that same strategy results in lower emissions. I mean, why are we arguing? That's a win-win, and there's clear evidence that some of the newer energy strategies that we've been implementing in the United States, they have made energy more affordable and reduced emissions, and not slightly. I'm talking huge reductions. and emissions that allows the United States to be the global leader in emissions reduction over the last about 15 years. And so, you know, I just think it's important instead of just talking about it as this macro issue. Let's talk about the components of it. Let's break it down into bite-sized pieces. And we've had a lot of success in bringing people together and making progress and changing laws that truly advances these objectives of of having a cleaner environment, reducing emissions, setting new global standards on technology and best practices. And these are all wins. And of course, just the resilience of our communities that are and our ecosystem is fantastic and so I've enjoyed the opportunity to work on it and I just hope we can keep trying to move hopefully after the presidential election and all the politics that goes along with it get back down to some degree of sanity and make progress on these issues instead of just making everything a food fight.",
